-- Q3262: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The arrangement is always a service contract because the software runs on the vendor''s servers and the contingent clause is irrelevant","Any CCA involving internet access is automatically classified as a service contract since the vendor controls hosting","The arrangement contains a software license only if Danforth currently operates the software on its own infrastructure","The arrangement likely contains a software license because Danforth has a contractual right to take possession and can feasibly run it"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASU 2018-15, a CCA contains a software license if two conditions are met: (1) the customer has the contractual right to take possession without significant penalty, and (2) it is feasible for the customer to run the software independently. Danforth''s take-possession clause satisfies the first condition, and technical capability satisfies the second.
Wrong (A): Current hosting location does not determine classification. The contractual right to take possession is what matters under ASU 2018-15, not where the software currently runs.
Wrong (B): Internet delivery alone does not determine classification. The two-condition test under ASU 2018-15 must be applied regardless of delivery method.
Wrong (C): ASU 2018-15 looks at the contractual right and feasibility to take possession, not whether the customer currently operates the software on-premises.$EXPL$
