-- Q3263: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Are always expensed regardless of project stage or asset qualification","Are never applicable to any software development projects under GAAP","May be capitalized if the project qualifies as an asset under ASC 835-20","Are capitalized only for public companies, not private entities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 835-20, interest costs may be capitalized for qualifying assets during the period of development. Internal-use software under active development in the application development stage can qualify if the development period is substantial.
Wrong (A): Interest costs are not always expensed. ASC 835-20 specifically permits capitalization for qualifying assets during development, including internal-use software projects.
Wrong (B): Software projects are not excluded from interest capitalization. ASC 835-20 applies to any qualifying asset with a substantial development period.
Wrong (D): The interest capitalization rules under ASC 835-20 apply to both public and private companies without distinction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3263;

-- Q3264: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Write off the $150,000 remaining book value as the old software provides no future benefit","Continue amortizing the old software over its original remaining useful life","Transfer the $150,000 net book value to the new cloud computing prepaid asset account","Reclassify the $150,000 as a deferred charge and amortize over the SaaS term"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): When a company abandons on-premises software in favor of a cloud solution, the remaining unamortized book value must be written off as a loss in the period of abandonment under ASC 350-40. The old asset no longer provides future economic benefit.
Wrong (B): Continuing amortization is incorrect because an abandoned asset has no remaining useful life. Once the software is no longer in use, it must be derecognized.
Wrong (C): The old software cost cannot be transferred to a new SaaS arrangement. The new cloud costs are accounted for separately under ASU 2018-15.
Wrong (D): There is no basis to reclassify abandoned software as a deferred charge. The asset must be written off when it ceases to provide future benefit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3264;

-- Q3265: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is developed primarily for sale or licensing to external customers","Is developed or obtained for internal needs with no plan to market it externally","Is hosted exclusively in a cloud-based computing environment","Must be purchased from a third-party vendor rather than built in-house"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-40, internal-use software is software acquired, internally developed, or modified solely to meet the entity''s internal needs. There must be no substantive plan to market the software externally during development.
Wrong (A): Software sold to external customers falls under ASC 985-20 (software to be sold), not the internal-use software guidance in ASC 350-40.
Wrong (C): Internal-use software can be on-premises, cloud-based, or hosted in any environment. The delivery model does not define internal-use classification.
Wrong (D): Internal-use software may be built in-house, purchased from a vendor, or modified from existing software. The source does not determine the classification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3265;

-- Q3267: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
