-- Q3268: Advanced Lease Transactions [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The leaseback is classified as an operating lease rather than a finance lease","The transfer of the asset qualifies as a sale under ASC 606 revenue recognition criteria","The lease term is less than 75% of the remaining economic life of the asset","The seller-lessee pays cash consideration for an embedded buyback option"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-40, a sale-leaseback is recognized as a sale only if the transfer meets the criteria for a sale under ASC 606, meaning control of the asset has transferred to the buyer-lessor. If control has not transferred, the transaction is a financing arrangement.
Wrong (A): Leaseback classification as operating or finance does not determine whether the initial transfer qualifies as a sale. The sale test under ASC 606 is the threshold requirement.
Wrong (C): The 75% economic life test relates to lease classification, not to whether a sale-leaseback qualifies as a sale under ASC 842-40.
Wrong (D): A buyback option would typically prevent sale recognition by indicating control has not transferred, rather than enabling it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3268;

-- Q3269: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The full gain or loss on the sale immediately in the period of the transaction","Only a gain on the sale; any losses on the transaction must be deferred","A gain or loss only to the extent not related to the right-of-use asset retained","No gain or loss because the transaction is recorded at the asset''s book value"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 842-40, the seller-lessee recognizes a gain or loss only on the portion of the asset effectively transferred to the buyer-lessor, excluding the portion related to the right-of-use asset retained through the leaseback. If the transaction is at fair value, the full gain may be recognized.
Wrong (A): The full gain is not recognized immediately. ASC 842-40 requires the seller-lessee to exclude the portion of gain related to the retained right-of-use asset.
Wrong (B): There is no asymmetric treatment of gains versus losses. Both gains and losses are subject to the same partial recognition rule under ASC 842-40.
Wrong (D): The transaction is not recorded at book value. Pryor sold the building for $3.5 million against a $2.4 million carrying value, and the resulting gain is partially recognized.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3269;

-- Q3270: Advanced Lease Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A separate contract if the additional right is priced at its standalone price","Always a modification of the existing lease regardless of pricing terms","A termination of the original lease followed by a new replacement lease","An impairment event requiring reassessment of the existing right-of-use asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 842-10-25-8, a lease modification that adds the right to use an additional asset is treated as a separate contract if two conditions are met: (1) the scope increases and (2) the consideration is commensurate with the standalone price. Otherwise, the existing lease is modified.
Wrong (B): Not all modifications are treated as modifications of the existing lease. When the additional right is priced at standalone value, the new right is accounted for as a separate contract.
Wrong (C): A lease modification adding scope does not terminate the original lease. The original lease continues, and the new right is either separated or folded into the modification.
Wrong (D): Adding a new right-of-use asset is not an impairment event. Impairment applies when the carrying amount of an existing ROU asset exceeds its recoverable amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3270;

-- Q3272: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A partial termination with proportional reduction of ROU asset and lease liability","An adjustment to the discount rate only with no change to the ROU asset","A new replacement lease that supersedes the original lease agreement entirely","No accounting change is recorded until the modification date formally takes effect"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 842-10-25-15, a modification that decreases the scope of a lease is a partial termination. The lessee proportionally reduces the ROU asset and lease liability, recognizing any difference as a gain or loss in the period of modification.
Wrong (B): A partial termination requires adjustment to both the lease liability and the ROU asset, not merely a discount rate change. The scope reduction triggers proportional derecognition.
Wrong (C): Reducing scope does not create a new lease. The original lease continues at the reduced scope, and the lessee adjusts existing balances rather than starting fresh.
Wrong (D): The accounting change is recorded at the effective date of the modification. Delaying recognition would misstate the lease obligation and ROU asset.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3272;

-- Q3274: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The original lease classification assigned by the head lease lessor","The sublease terms evaluated against the right-of-use asset, not the underlying asset","Always as an operating lease regardless of the sublease terms or duration","The credit quality and financial standing of the sublessee tenant"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-10-25-2, a sublessor classifies the sublease by reference to the right-of-use asset arising from the head lease, not the underlying asset. Standard lease classification criteria are applied to the ROU asset as the underlying asset for this evaluation.
Wrong (A): The original head lease classification does not automatically carry through to the sublease. The sublessor must independently classify the sublease against the ROU asset.
Wrong (C): Subleases are not automatically classified as operating leases. They must be evaluated using the standard ASC 842 classification criteria applied to the ROU asset.
Wrong (D): The sublessee''s credit quality may affect collectibility assessments but does not determine the lease classification under ASC 842.$EXPL$
