-- Q3295: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The contribution reduces both the funded status liability and pension expense by $1,000,000 equally","The contribution increases plan assets to $9,500,000, reducing the funded status liability to $500,000, but pension expense is unaffected","The contribution has no balance sheet effect because employer contributions are expensed immediately as pension cost","The contribution reduces plan assets because the cash leaves the employer''s control and enters the trust"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715, the $1,000,000 contribution increases plan assets from $8,500,000 to $9,500,000, reducing the funded status liability from $1,500,000 to $500,000. Pension expense is unaffected because its components (service cost, interest cost, expected return, amortizations) are determined by actuarial assumptions, not cash contributions.
Wrong (A): Contributions do not reduce pension expense. Pension expense is calculated using actuarial assumptions and is independent of employer funding decisions.
Wrong (C): The contribution does affect the balance sheet. It increases plan assets and thereby reduces the net pension liability reported as funded status.
Wrong (D): Contributions increase plan assets, not reduce them. The cash transfer to the trust adds to the plan''s asset pool.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3295;

-- Q3297: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["10% of the greater of the PBO or market-related value of plan assets","5% of the projected benefit obligation at the beginning of the period","10% of the lesser of the PBO or the fair value of plan assets","Any amount of unrecognized gain or loss must be amortized immediately"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-24, the corridor is 10% of the greater of the beginning PBO or the market-related value of plan assets. Only cumulative unrecognized net gains or losses exceeding this corridor are subject to minimum amortization over the average remaining service period.
Wrong (B): The threshold is 10%, not 5%, and it uses the greater of the PBO or market-related plan assets, not the PBO alone.
Wrong (C): The corridor uses the greater of the two amounts, not the lesser. Using the lesser would create a narrower corridor and trigger amortization more frequently than intended.
Wrong (D): Immediate amortization of any amount is not required. The corridor provides a buffer against annual volatility, allowing gains and losses within it to remain unrecognized.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3297;

-- Q3299: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The expected long-term rate of return on the pension plan''s invested assets","The company''s weighted average cost of equity capital from its investors","The risk-free Treasury rate matching the duration of benefit payments","The rates at which pension benefits could be effectively settled using high-quality bonds"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 715-30-35-43, the discount rate for measuring the PBO should reflect the rates at which pension benefits could be effectively settled. In practice, this means yields on high-quality corporate bonds (typically AA-rated) with durations matching expected benefit payment timing.
Wrong (A): The expected return on plan assets measures anticipated investment performance, not the time value of the obligation. It is a separate pension cost component.
Wrong (B): The company''s cost of equity reflects shareholder return expectations and is unrelated to the discount rate used to measure pension obligations under ASC 715.
Wrong (C): The risk-free Treasury rate understates the appropriate discount rate. ASC 715 requires high-quality corporate bond rates, which include a credit spread above Treasuries.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3299;

-- Q3301: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["On a pay-as-you-go cash basis recognizing cost only when benefits are paid","On an accrual basis over employees'' service periods using the APBO measure","Only when individual retirees submit claims for reimbursement of health costs","As contingent liabilities disclosed in the footnotes but not recorded on the books"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-60, postretirement benefits other than pensions are accounted for on an accrual basis, similar to pension accounting. The employer accrues the cost over employees'' service periods using the accumulated postretirement benefit obligation (APBO).
Wrong (A): Pay-as-you-go cash basis accounting is not permitted for OPEB under ASC 715-60. The obligation must be accrued over the employees'' active service periods.
Wrong (C): Waiting for claims to be submitted would be a cash-basis approach. ASC 715-60 requires recognition of the obligation as employees earn the right to benefits.
Wrong (D): OPEB obligations are recognized liabilities, not merely contingent. They meet the criteria for accrual because the obligation is probable and reasonably estimable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3301;

-- Q3302: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The EPBO is the total expected cost for current employees; the APBO is the portion attributed to service to date","The APBO includes more projected future service years than the EPBO in its calculation","The EPBO and APBO are identical measures that differ only in name and presentation","The EPBO applies exclusively to pension plans while the APBO applies to health benefits"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-60-20, the EPBO represents the total expected cost of all postretirement benefits over an employee''s retirement period. The APBO is the portion of the EPBO attributed to service rendered to date, prorated based on service to date versus total expected service to the full eligibility date.
Wrong (B): The APBO does not include more future service than the EPBO. The APBO is always less than or equal to the EPBO, as it reflects only the service-to-date portion.
Wrong (C): They are not identical. The EPBO reflects the full lifetime obligation regardless of service rendered, while the APBO grows incrementally with each year of service until it equals the EPBO at full eligibility.
Wrong (D): Both the EPBO and APBO are OPEB measures under ASC 715-60. Pension plans use the PBO and ABO, which are distinct measures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3302;

-- Q3307: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a component of other income and expense below the operating income line","Within the same line items as other employee compensation costs in operating expense","As a separate line item presented below operating income on the income statement","In other comprehensive income rather than on the income statement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASU 2017-07 (codified in ASC 715), service cost is the only component of net periodic pension cost that must be reported within operating expense, in the same line items as other employee compensation costs.
Wrong (A): Service cost is not reported in other income/expense. ASU 2017-07 specifically requires it to be classified as an operating expense alongside other compensation.
Wrong (C): Service cost is not presented as a separate line below operating income. It is included within operating expenses, not segregated from them.
Wrong (D): Service cost is recognized on the income statement, not in other comprehensive income. OCI is used for items like actuarial gains and losses, not service cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3307;

-- Q3309: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Decrease the APBO due to lower projected health care utilization by retirees","Decrease service cost only while leaving the APBO obligation unchanged","Have no effect on OPEB measurements since the trend rate is a secondary assumption","Increase the APBO and the net periodic postretirement benefit cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 715-60, a higher health care cost trend rate increases projected future health care costs, which raises both the APBO and the service cost and interest cost components of net periodic postretirement benefit cost. The trend rate is one of the most sensitive OPEB assumptions.
Wrong (A): A higher trend rate increases projected costs, which increases the APBO. It does not decrease the obligation.
Wrong (B): Both service cost and the APBO are affected by changes in the health care cost trend rate. The rate influences all projected future benefit costs, not just one component.
Wrong (C): The health care cost trend rate is a primary OPEB assumption with significant impact. ASC 715-60 requires disclosure of its sensitivity to one-percentage-point changes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3309;

-- Q3310: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["GASB requires dual reporting because federal law mandates it for all governmental entities regardless of size","Fund statements use modified accrual for fiscal accountability while government-wide use full accrual for operational accountability","The reconciliation exists only to identify accounting errors and should show zero differences if properly prepared","GASB requires two sets solely because government-wide statements cannot present fund-level detail"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): GASB 34 established the dual-perspective model. Fund statements use the current financial resources measurement focus and modified accrual basis to show fiscal accountability, while government-wide statements use economic resources and full accrual to show operational accountability. The reconciliation bridges these systematic differences.
Wrong (A): The dual reporting requirement comes from GASB Statement No. 34, not federal law. GASB sets accounting standards for state and local governments.
Wrong (C): The reconciliation does not identify errors. It explains systematic differences caused by different measurement focuses and bases of accounting applied to the same transactions.
Wrong (D): The difference is conceptual, not presentational. The two statement sets use different measurement focuses and accounting bases, not merely different levels of detail.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3310;

-- Q3311: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are subtracted from the government-wide net position in the reconciliation","No adjustment is needed because both statements treat them the same way","They are added back because they are capitalized rather than expensed government-wide","They are reclassified as revenue on the government-wide statement of activities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 34, paragraphs 77-78, capital outlays recorded as expenditures in governmental funds must be reclassified as capital assets on government-wide statements. The reconciliation adds back the expenditure because government-wide statements capitalize and depreciate these assets over their useful lives.
Wrong (A): Capital outlays are added back, not subtracted. The fund expenditure is reversed and replaced with a capital asset on the government-wide statements.
Wrong (B): An adjustment is required because the two statement sets treat capital outlays differently. Funds expense them immediately while government-wide statements capitalize them.
Wrong (D): Capital outlays are not reclassified as revenue. They are reclassified from expenditures to capital assets on the statement of net position.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3311;

-- Q3312: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Governmental funds do not own or hold title to any capital assets","Depreciation is an optional accounting choice for governmental entities","Governmental funds use current financial resources focus which excludes long-term assets","Capital assets are reported exclusively in fiduciary funds instead"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 34, paragraphs 18-20, governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus, which reports only current assets and liabilities. Capital assets and depreciation are excluded. Government-wide statements use economic resources focus and report depreciation.
Wrong (A): Governmental funds do own capital assets. The assets are simply not reported in fund statements due to the current financial resources measurement focus.
Wrong (B): Depreciation is not optional on government-wide statements. GASB 34 requires capital assets to be depreciated over their useful lives under full accrual accounting.
Wrong (D): Capital assets of governmental activities are reported on government-wide statements, not in fiduciary funds. Fiduciary funds hold resources in trust for external parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3312;

-- Q3313: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Left as revenue on the government-wide statements without any adjustment","Removed because they represent a liability rather than revenue government-wide","Added directly to net position as a permanent equity increase","Reclassified as investment income on the government-wide statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, paragraph 77, bond proceeds reported as other financing sources in governmental funds must be reclassified as a long-term liability on government-wide statements. Borrowing creates an asset (cash) and an offsetting liability, not revenue.
Wrong (A): Bond proceeds cannot remain as revenue. At the government-wide level under economic resources focus, borrowing creates a liability, not a revenue source.
Wrong (C): Bond proceeds do not increase net position. The cash received is exactly offset by the long-term liability, producing no net change in net position.
Wrong (D): Bond proceeds are debt issuance, not investment income. Reclassifying them as investment income would mischaracterize the nature of the transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3313;

-- Q3314: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The payment is an expense on both statements because debt repayment always reduces net position equally","The payment has no effect on either statement because bond principal payments are off-balance-sheet","Fund balance decreases by $5,000,000 but net position increases by $5,000,000 as eliminating debt creates value","The payment is a fund expenditure reducing fund balance but a liability reduction on government-wide with no expense"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 34, the $5,000,000 principal payment is an expenditure reducing fund balance on fund statements. On government-wide statements, it reduces bonds payable rather than being an expense. Cash decreases $5,000,000 and liability decreases $5,000,000, leaving net position unchanged.
Wrong (A): The payment is not an expense on government-wide statements. Debt repayment reduces a liability, not an expense, under the economic resources measurement focus.
Wrong (B): The payment has substantial effects on both statements. It reduces fund balance on fund statements and reduces the bond liability on government-wide statements.
Wrong (C): Net position does not increase. Both cash and the bond liability decrease by equal amounts, producing zero net change in net position from this transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3314;

-- Q3316: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Adds the amount to revenue because full accrual recognizes it when earned","Subtracts the amount from net position as a permanent reduction","Leaves it as a deferred inflow on both statement sets without change","Converts the deferred inflow into a long-term liability on government-wide"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34, paragraphs 77-79, revenue deferred in governmental funds because it is unavailable must be recognized as revenue on government-wide statements under full accrual. The reconciliation converts these deferred inflows to revenue, increasing net position.
Wrong (B): The adjustment increases net position by recognizing deferred revenue, not decreasing it. Converting unavailable revenue to recognized revenue is a positive adjustment.
Wrong (C): The deferred inflow does not carry over to government-wide statements unchanged. Full accrual recognizes the revenue when earned regardless of collection timing.
Wrong (D): Property taxes receivable are not liabilities. They represent revenue that was deferred under modified accrual but is recognized under full accrual.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3316;

-- Q3317: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Subtracted from governmental activities as an inter-fund elimination adjustment","Added to governmental activities because internal service funds predominantly serve them","Reported with business-type activities alongside enterprise fund operations","Eliminated entirely from the government-wide statements in consolidation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, paragraph 62, internal service funds primarily serve governmental activities and their net position is typically consolidated with governmental activities in the government-wide reconciliation. Their operations support governmental programs such as motor pool, printing, or IT.
Wrong (A): Internal service fund net position is added, not subtracted. Consolidating these fund balances into governmental activities reflects their support of governmental programs.
Wrong (C): Internal service funds are not reported with business-type activities. Unlike enterprise funds that serve external customers, internal service funds support internal governmental operations.
Wrong (D): Internal service funds are not eliminated entirely. Their balances are consolidated into governmental activities because they are integral to governmental programs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3317;

-- Q3319: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A long-term liability reported in full on the governmental fund balance sheet","Deferred outflows of resources recognized over the employees'' service periods","An expense accrued over each employee''s service period in fund statements","An expenditure only when amounts are due and payable with available resources"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 34, paragraph 80, compensated absences in governmental funds are recognized as expenditures only when they mature and become due and payable with currently available resources. Only the $85,000 currently due portion would be recognized.
Wrong (A): Governmental funds do not report long-term liabilities. Under modified accrual, only the currently due portion is recognized; the full $1.2 million appears only on government-wide statements.
Wrong (B): Compensated absences are not classified as deferred outflows of resources. They are either fund expenditures when matured or long-term liabilities on government-wide statements.
Wrong (C): Governmental funds do not accrue expenses over service periods. That is the full accrual treatment used on government-wide statements, not the modified accrual approach used in fund statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3319;
